  "diagnostics": "Retain all ten exploratory runs and 70 runs before correcting last-viewport scroll settling. content-visibility initially left the generated last screenshot at image 392; final repeated settling verifies the bottom gap and image 400 visibility. Timing/production code is unchanged between groups. Both timing groups are excluded from final claims; failed, slow and non-improving measurements are retained.",
  "changes": [
    "Use per-load attached containers and an internal AbortController; superseded or destroyed loads cannot overwrite active DOM/state or emit late error/rendered events.",
    "Abort obsolete network work; discard late parser/render results and reset scroll for new documents.",
    "Scope resource caches to the parsed document and relation ID/part, preserving header/body distinctions and font keys. Repeated image/font/numbering references share their resource promise.",
    "Release all session-owned resource URLs on replacement, cancellation, failure and destruction; release resources which resolve after cancellation.",
    "Pass snapshotted input bytes directly to docx-preview/JSZip; preserve input slices and mutation safety without a Blob/FileReader round trip. Blob input retains its existing renderer path."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Same-engine old/new engineering comparison, not a frontend peer/SOTA ranking.",
    "The 8.31x case is generated and repeats one 256x256 PNG 400 times over 20 pages; it is not a general DOCX speed claim. Blob input is a separate representation of the same generated file.",
    "Timing excludes image decode readiness, paint, initial file I/O and module loading; no first-visible-time or peak-memory claim.",
    "Already-started docx-preview parser/render work may finish in a detached container; no worker-level interruption or on-demand document rendering is added.",
    "Real rich-docx-images has no stable gain. The tables control remains below 10 ms and gets no speed claim.",
    "Resource-loader integration is verified against docx-preview 0.3.7. Actual documents cover images, tables and repeated references; numbering/font URL teardown also has focused mocked unit coverage.",
    "Visual equivalence covers first and last viewports, while canonical DOM and resource payload checks cover the complete document; not every page has a pixel comparison."
  ],
